19
00:01:20,961 --> 00:01:23,320
These are machines like no other.

20
00:01:23,321 --> 00:01:24,960
These are the world's biggest machines.

21
00:01:24,961 --> 00:01:28,960
And many of them are built to fly.

22
00:01:28,961 --> 00:01:31,960
Ever since the Wright brothers took flight in the first

23
00:01:31,961 --> 00:01:36,960
successful airplane in 1903, we've been on a never-ending

24
00:01:36,961 --> 00:01:40,960
quest to thrust more payload into the sky.

25
00:01:40,961 --> 00:01:43,960
From Howard Hughes' spruce goose flying boat to the ever-dependable

26
00:01:43,961 --> 00:01:47,960
B-52 bomber, up to today's airline workhorse, the Boeing

27
00:01:47,961 --> 00:01:53,960
747, we've been driven to build them big.

28
00:01:53,961 --> 00:02:01,460
big, and no one more so than the Russians.

29
00:02:01,560 --> 00:02:06,900
The Antonov 225 is the world's largest airplane.

30
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:09,100
Its dimensions are staggering.

31
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:12,520
It's nearly a football field in length from nose to tail,

32
00:02:12,620 --> 00:02:16,420
and its wingspan stretches an amazing 291 feet.

33
00:02:20,420 --> 00:02:23,960
Built originally by the Soviets in 1988,

34
00:02:23,961 --> 00:02:27,460
to transport their prototype space shuttle.

35
00:02:27,461 --> 00:02:30,900
It's used today as a supercargo carrier,

36
00:02:30,901 --> 00:02:34,300
capable of carrying twice the payload of a Boeing 747.

37
00:02:38,600 --> 00:02:41,960
Any of the numbers you use to describe the AN-225

38
00:02:41,961 --> 00:02:44,360
come under the heading of superlatives.

39
00:02:44,361 --> 00:02:47,500
For instance, it was the first aircraft

40
00:02:47,501 --> 00:02:51,200
to fly that took off at a gross weight of greater

41
00:02:51,201 --> 00:02:52,420
than 1 million pounds.

42
00:02:53,960 --> 00:02:57,960
Everything about this jumbo jet is massive.

43
00:02:58,060 --> 00:03:03,400
It's powered by six giant turbofan jets,

44
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:08,400
each capable of pumping out 51,590 pounds of thrust.

45
00:03:11,020 --> 00:03:14,020
The Soviets had to incorporate many innovations into its

46
00:03:14,120 --> 00:03:18,620
design because of its size, including a 32-wheel steerable

47
00:03:18,720 --> 00:03:20,920
landing gear.

48
00:03:20,921 --> 00:03:25,920
In addition to the nose wheel section being maneuverable,

49
00:03:26,020 --> 00:03:31,020
the rear parts of the main landing gear are also steerable.

50
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:35,940
The main landing gear consists of, on each side,

51
00:03:36,040 --> 00:03:39,880
seven units of twin wheel assemblies.

52
00:03:39,980 --> 00:03:43,780
The last four on each side are steerable.

53
00:03:43,880 --> 00:03:47,380
And having the last four trucks, as we call them,

54
00:03:47,480 --> 00:03:49,920
of the main landing gear assembly steerable,

55
00:03:49,921 --> 00:03:54,120
steerable on the aircraft makes it able to be turned

56
00:03:54,220 --> 00:03:56,320
in a relatively small turning radius

57
00:03:56,420 --> 00:03:59,520
so that you don't have to build massively large taxiways

58
00:03:59,620 --> 00:04:04,060
and parking aprons for this very large aircraft.

59
00:04:04,160 --> 00:04:07,180
The giant also sports a wide split tail,

60
00:04:07,280 --> 00:04:09,480
designed to avoid the air turbulence created

61
00:04:09,580 --> 00:04:14,380
by large piggyback loads, like the space shuttle.

62
00:04:14,480 --> 00:04:16,580
If you have a single tail aircraft,

63
00:04:16,680 --> 00:04:19,480
there's some turbulence that is set up

64
00:04:19,481 --> 00:04:23,280
by the airflow around your cargo that you're carrying

65
00:04:23,281 --> 00:04:28,580
on the back of the aircraft by making a double tail

66
00:04:28,581 --> 00:04:32,520
where the two tail sections are outboard,

67
00:04:32,521 --> 00:04:35,120
then the turbulent airflow goes between them.

68
00:04:35,121 --> 00:04:38,300
Then that allows for a smooth aerodynamic flow

69
00:04:38,301 --> 00:04:40,840
over the vertical fin and rudder assemblies

70
00:04:40,841 --> 00:04:43,140
on either side of the horizontal stabilizer.

71
00:04:43,141 --> 00:04:52,140
The AN-225 made its international debut in a style befitting its

72
00:04:52,141 --> 00:04:57,480
dimensions when it landed at the 1989 Paris Air Show with a Soviet

73
00:04:57,481 --> 00:05:03,260
space shuttle on its back. From the beginning, it caused a

74
00:05:03,261 --> 00:05:06,800
sensation. However, its glory days were

75
00:05:06,801 --> 00:05:10,440
short-lived. After the fall of the Soviet Union

76
00:05:10,441 --> 00:05:15,440
the plane was without a mission. It was grounded in Kiev and

77
00:05:15,441 --> 00:05:20,440
scavenged for parts. Then, after nearly six years,

78
00:05:20,441 --> 00:05:24,440
the only AN-225 ever built was rescued from the scrap heap by

79
00:05:24,441 --> 00:05:28,440
the Antonov Group, who put $20 million into refurbishing it as

80
00:05:28,441 --> 00:05:33,440
a cargo carrier. Today, their investment is paying

81
00:05:33,441 --> 00:05:39,440
off, carrying everything from heavy construction machinery to

82
00:05:39,441 --> 00:05:44,240
machinery to oil drilling equipment to fleets of cars,

83
00:05:44,241 --> 00:05:46,740
the future once again looks bright for this giant of the

84
00:05:46,741 --> 00:05:52,740
skies. Crucial to the success of the

85
00:05:52,741 --> 00:05:56,740
Antonov and other big powerful planes was the development of

86
00:05:56,741 --> 00:06:00,740
the jet engine. Today the engines are becoming

87
00:06:00,741 --> 00:06:08,740
giants themselves. Dr. Hans von Ohain of Germany.

88
00:06:08,741 --> 00:06:12,740
and Sir Frank Whittle of Great Britain are recognized as co-inventors

89
00:06:12,741 --> 00:06:17,280
of the jet engine. Ironically, they researched

90
00:06:17,281 --> 00:06:20,740
separately from each other during the 1930s and knew nothing of

91
00:06:20,741 --> 00:06:24,480
each other's concepts, yet both produced functional jet planes

92
00:06:24,481 --> 00:06:29,480
by the early 1940s. Jet engines operate by sucking

93
00:06:29,481 --> 00:06:33,480
in air with a fan. They then compress the air inside

94
00:06:33,481 --> 00:06:37,540
the engine and spray it with fuel, which is ignited.

95
00:06:37,541 --> 00:06:40,380
The burning gas expands and blasts out the back of the

96
00:06:40,381 --> 00:06:42,780
engine, thrusting the aircraft forward.

97
00:06:45,780 --> 00:06:48,780
General Electric has produced the world's largest and most

98
00:06:48,781 --> 00:06:54,100
powerful jet engine, the GE90-115B.

99
00:06:54,101 --> 00:06:56,100
We've broken the world's record twice for

100
00:06:56,101 --> 00:07:01,100
demonstrated thrust, most recently demonstrating 127,900

101
00:07:01,101 --> 00:07:05,340
pounds of thrust. That's equivalent to over 100,000

102
00:07:05,341 --> 00:07:07,100
horsepower.

103
00:07:07,101 --> 00:07:09,900
The massive engine was developed to power twin-engine

104
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:13,240
airliners like the new Boeing 777.

105
00:07:13,340 --> 00:07:15,180
Many experts believe twin-engine aircraft

106
00:07:15,280 --> 00:07:19,580
may prove to be the wave of the future in the airline business

107
00:07:19,680 --> 00:07:21,480
because they're less expensive to operate

108
00:07:21,580 --> 00:07:22,920
than four-engine planes.

109
00:07:26,980 --> 00:07:28,720
Even veterans of the aviation business

110
00:07:28,820 --> 00:07:32,160
are amazed by the engine size.

111
00:07:32,260 --> 00:07:33,520
They're pretty much awestruck.

112
00:07:33,620 --> 00:07:36,060
A lot of them really just are kind of silent and just

113
00:07:36,061 --> 00:07:39,300
and just big eyes and this look on their faces,

114
00:07:39,400 --> 00:07:40,760
it's just almost incredible to see.

115
00:07:40,860 --> 00:07:42,760
It's so much larger than any other engine

116
00:07:42,860 --> 00:07:44,560
they've ever seen before.

117
00:07:44,660 --> 00:07:46,100
Key to the success of the engine

118
00:07:46,200 --> 00:07:51,300
is its giant 128-inch diameter fan, one of the largest ever

119
00:07:51,400 --> 00:07:52,620
constructed for a jet engine.

120
00:07:55,720 --> 00:07:57,920
The fan sucks in enormous amounts of air

121
00:07:58,020 --> 00:08:00,220
that allow the engine to generate its record-breaking

122
00:08:00,320 --> 00:08:02,820
thrust.

123
00:08:02,920 --> 00:08:05,660
Most jet engine fan blades are made of extremely strong

124
00:08:05,661 --> 00:08:08,500
titanium. However, constructing a fan

125
00:08:08,501 --> 00:08:11,900
this large entirely of titanium would have made the engine

126
00:08:11,901 --> 00:08:14,900
impossibly heavy. General Electric solved this

127
00:08:14,901 --> 00:08:18,400
problem by designing new fan blades made of lighter carbon

128
00:08:18,401 --> 00:08:21,700
fiber polymeric materials and then giving them a titanium

129
00:08:21,701 --> 00:08:27,320
leading edge for strength. The shape of the fan blades is

130
00:08:27,321 --> 00:08:31,420
also a key ingredient in creating the engine's enormous power.

131
00:08:31,421 --> 00:08:35,420
Older engines used simple straight up and down fan blades.

132
00:08:35,421 --> 00:08:40,160
But the GE90 is equipped with aerodynamic swept wing blades.

133
00:08:40,161 --> 00:08:41,660
The unique shape of the fan blade

134
00:08:41,661 --> 00:08:44,760
is key to the technology of the engine as well.

135
00:08:44,761 --> 00:08:48,460
And that 3D shape, that beautiful 3D shape that you see,

136
00:08:48,461 --> 00:08:51,980
draws in more air than just a straight up and down

137
00:08:51,981 --> 00:08:53,580
or radial blade.

138
00:08:53,581 --> 00:08:56,380
It actually draws in more air in the same circumference.

139
00:08:56,381 --> 00:08:58,380
And that extra air gives us that extra little bit of thrust

140
00:08:58,381 --> 00:09:01,180
that we need.

141
00:09:01,181 --> 00:09:03,980
Before the engine could be certified for flight,

142
00:09:03,981 --> 00:09:06,620
It had to be put through a battery of demanding tests.

143
00:09:09,520 --> 00:09:13,620
The testing is to assure that the engine can fly

144
00:09:13,720 --> 00:09:16,020
in any environment safely.

145
00:09:16,120 --> 00:09:18,120
We look at any extreme environments

146
00:09:18,220 --> 00:09:20,800
and make the testing more severe than what you'll see

147
00:09:20,900 --> 00:09:23,540
in operation on an aircraft.

148
00:09:23,640 --> 00:09:25,500
We have water ingestion testing.

149
00:09:25,600 --> 00:09:28,680
We take in about 4 and 1 1 tons of water a minute

150
00:09:28,780 --> 00:09:31,180
and show that the engine can continue to operate

151
00:09:31,181 --> 00:09:36,480
and not stall, taking in that kind of volume of water.

152
00:09:36,580 --> 00:09:38,780
We also do hail ingestion testing.

153
00:09:38,880 --> 00:09:42,220
We have several little cannons that shoot hail into the engine.

154
00:09:42,320 --> 00:09:46,220
They shoot about 3 quarters of a ton of hail in 30 seconds.

155
00:09:48,840 --> 00:09:51,340
Nothing was left to chance.

156
00:09:51,440 --> 00:09:53,640
Technicians tried to recreate and analyze

157
00:09:53,740 --> 00:09:57,440
every possible problem that could happen to the engine.

158
00:09:57,540 --> 00:09:59,680
An engine, when it's operating on an aircraft,

159
00:09:59,681 --> 00:10:01,180
takes in a lot of air.

160
00:10:01,181 --> 00:10:03,320
It can also take in anything else in the environment,

161
00:10:03,321 --> 00:10:04,520
such as a bird.

162
00:10:04,521 --> 00:10:08,180
So we're required, per the FAA, to shoot bird carcasses

163
00:10:08,181 --> 00:10:10,520
into the engine and to show that we can continue

164
00:10:10,521 --> 00:10:15,520
to sustain operation even after taking in the bird carcasses.

165
00:10:15,521 --> 00:10:18,140
Perhaps the most important test of the engine

166
00:10:18,141 --> 00:10:21,340
