167
00:10:21,341 --> 00:10:23,340
Since the early days of aviation,

168
00:10:23,341 --> 00:10:25,040
special planes have been designated

169
00:10:25,041 --> 00:10:27,340
for testing new equipment.

170
00:10:27,341 --> 00:10:30,980
GE actually owns a flying test bed, a 747 aircraft,

171
00:10:30,981 --> 00:10:33,580
where we test engines prior to doing flight tests

172
00:10:33,581 --> 00:10:35,140
on any new aircraft.

173
00:10:35,141 --> 00:10:39,580
In the case of the GE-9115B, we had this huge GE-90 engine

174
00:10:39,581 --> 00:10:42,480
on the aircraft with three smaller engines.

175
00:10:42,481 --> 00:10:45,000
Amazingly, the engine proved powerful enough

176
00:10:45,001 --> 00:10:48,200
to fly a giant 747 by itself.

177
00:10:48,201 --> 00:10:49,800
We had these three smaller engines

178
00:10:49,801 --> 00:10:52,140
that we actually cut the power to all those engines

179
00:10:52,141 --> 00:10:57,340
and just flew it on the one GE-9115B engine.

180
00:10:57,341 --> 00:11:00,940
Another story about the flying test bed that's pretty amazing

181
00:11:01,040 --> 00:11:02,940
is the air that's pulled through the engine

182
00:11:03,040 --> 00:11:04,140
to generate the thrust.

183
00:11:04,240 --> 00:11:05,740
As it was exiting the engine, it

184
00:11:05,840 --> 00:11:08,780
was actually moving 400-pound rocks that were kind of rolling

185
00:11:08,880 --> 00:11:10,080
behind the engine.

186
00:11:10,180 --> 00:11:12,680
I don't think anybody expected to see that.

187
00:11:12,780 --> 00:11:14,760
After two years in development,

188
00:11:14,860 --> 00:11:19,600
a $21 million engine is now a fixture on the Boeing 777,

189
00:11:19,700 --> 00:11:24,540
ushering in a new era for twin-engine airliners.

190
00:11:24,541 --> 00:11:30,340
airliners. Fixed wing aircraft builders

