It weighed 1.2 million pounds, which is 600 tons,

255
00:15:32,480 --> 00:15:34,620
when it was fully loaded with coal and water ready for the

256
00:15:34,720 --> 00:15:36,120
road.

257
00:15:36,220 --> 00:15:39,020
First put in operation in 1941,

258
00:15:39,021 --> 00:15:41,820
The big boys were built to pull heavy freight trains through the

259
00:15:41,821 --> 00:15:44,660
steep mountain passes of the Rockies.

260
00:15:44,661 --> 00:15:48,260
Whenever they pulled into town, they drew an admiring crowd.

261
00:15:48,261 --> 00:15:50,860
The ground would literally shake when a big boy rolled by,

262
00:15:50,861 --> 00:15:54,260
so they were very impressive machines to watch.

263
00:15:54,261 --> 00:15:56,980
Designed and constructed by the American Locomotive Company

264
00:15:56,981 --> 00:16:00,380
of Schenectady, New York, the big boy presented its builders

265
00:16:00,381 --> 00:16:03,720
with some major engineering challenges.

266
00:16:03,721 --> 00:16:07,620
Because of its enormous proposed length, with 16 wheels attached

267
00:16:07,621 --> 00:16:11,380
to a rigid frame, it would be impossible for it to stay on track

268
00:16:11,381 --> 00:16:15,380
while navigating sharp corners. It just wouldn't be flexible

269
00:16:15,381 --> 00:16:20,020
enough. To solve this problem, it was

270
00:16:20,021 --> 00:16:24,020
decided to make the locomotive articulated, meaning it would have

271
00:16:24,021 --> 00:16:27,860
two engines connected by a hinge pin between them.

272
00:16:27,861 --> 00:16:31,220
Both engines would be served by a single boiler.

273
00:16:31,221 --> 00:16:34,020
When negotiating a curve, the front engine would be able to

274
00:16:34,021 --> 00:16:37,540
swing out freely around the curve, and then the back end

275
00:16:37,541 --> 00:16:40,180
would follow.

276
00:16:40,280 --> 00:16:41,980
Supplying fuel to the giant locomotive

277
00:16:42,080 --> 00:16:44,040
also posed a problem.

278
00:16:44,140 --> 00:16:45,540
The tender of the big boy was designed

279
00:16:45,640 --> 00:16:48,380
to hold 28 tons of coal and 24,000 gallons of water.

280
00:16:48,480 --> 00:16:49,920
And obviously, a locomotive that big

281
00:16:50,020 --> 00:16:51,980
burned an enormous amount of coal and water

282
00:16:52,080 --> 00:16:54,020
in a trip up the mountains pulling a heavy train.

283
00:16:54,120 --> 00:16:56,600
Typically, it would burn about 8 to 10 tons of coal in an hour

284
00:16:56,700 --> 00:16:59,100
and go through about 8 to 10,000 gallons of water in an hour.

285
00:16:59,200 --> 00:17:00,900
Shoveling 8 to 10 tons of coal an hour

286
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:02,200
was more than any human could do.

287
00:17:02,300 --> 00:17:04,700
So there's a mechanical stoker in the engine.

288
00:17:04,800 --> 00:17:07,140
It's actually an Archimedes screw.

289
00:17:07,141 --> 00:17:09,980
And the screw is driven by a little small two-cylinder steam

290
00:17:09,981 --> 00:17:11,820
engine that sits in the tender.

291
00:17:11,821 --> 00:17:13,820
And the fireman controls the speed of that screw,

292
00:17:13,821 --> 00:17:16,980
and that pushes the coal into the firebox.

293
00:17:16,981 --> 00:17:20,120
The firebox itself was of epic proportions.

294
00:17:20,121 --> 00:17:22,920
Well, the firebox was one of the biggest ever put on a steam

295
00:17:22,921 --> 00:17:25,560
locomotive, which is fitting for a locomotive the size of the

296
00:17:25,561 --> 00:17:26,560
Big Boy.

297
00:17:26,561 --> 00:17:28,920
It's about 20 feet long, about 8 feet wide inside.

298
00:17:28,921 --> 00:17:34,600
There was literally an inferno inside a Big Boy firebox.

299
00:17:34,601 --> 00:17:40,400
Built at a cost of $265,000 in 1941, which would be $5 million

300
00:17:40,401 --> 00:17:43,700
today, the giant engine delivered all the power

301
00:17:43,701 --> 00:17:44,940
its designers had promised.

302
00:17:45,540 --> 00:17:49,360
The big boy produced in excess of 6,000 horsepower.

303
00:17:50,320 --> 00:17:54,640
And this was an exceptional amount of horsepower for the

304
00:17:54,641 --> 00:17:56,820
time, particularly for a steam engine.

305
00:17:57,380 --> 00:18:00,240
It also delivered speed that would be impressive even today.

306
00:18:00,820 --> 00:18:04,580
The speed of this locomotive was governed by the amount

307
00:18:04,581 --> 00:18:07,620
of tonnage that was tied onto the back end,

308
00:18:07,720 --> 00:18:11,520
but it could do in excess of 80 miles an hour.

309
00:18:11,620 --> 00:18:14,020
Yet for all its power, by 1960,

310
00:18:14,120 --> 00:18:16,920
the age of the steam locomotive had come to an end,

311
00:18:17,020 --> 00:18:19,920
and even the incredible big boy was forced into retirement.

312
00:18:24,140 --> 00:18:26,340
Well, steam engines were phased out, really,

313
00:18:26,440 --> 00:18:29,940
because better, more efficient technology came along.

314
00:18:30,040 --> 00:18:32,880
The diesel engine is better economically,

315
00:18:32,881 --> 00:18:34,540
And it was a lot easier to maintain.

316
00:18:37,520 --> 00:18:39,720
And today, there's a massive diesel locomotive

317
00:18:39,721 --> 00:18:42,320
that surpasses even the big boy.

318
00:18:42,321 --> 00:18:45,920
It's General Electric's AC6000.

319
00:18:45,921 --> 00:18:49,900
The AC6000 behind me is one of the largest locomotives

320
00:18:49,901 --> 00:18:51,200
on the rails today.

321
00:18:51,201 --> 00:18:54,340
It's 76 feet long, it's 16 feet high,

322
00:18:54,341 --> 00:18:56,200
and it's 10 and 1 1 feet wide.

323
00:18:56,201 --> 00:18:58,580
The locomotive with a full load of fuel

324
00:18:58,581 --> 00:19:03,080
weighs 425,000 pounds or 212 tons.

325
00:19:10,620 --> 00:19:14,060
This $2 million giant is quantum leaps ahead of the big boy

326
00:19:14,160 --> 00:19:15,400
in terms of technology.

327
00:19:15,500 --> 00:19:19,860
The AC6000 is powered by one large 16-cylinder diesel

328
00:19:19,960 --> 00:19:20,760
engine.

329
00:19:20,860 --> 00:19:24,100
The diesel engine produces 6,000 horsepower for traction.

330
00:19:24,200 --> 00:19:26,600
That's power that goes down to the rails.

331
00:19:26,601 --> 00:19:29,300
Everything about the engine is huge.

332
00:19:29,301 --> 00:19:32,100
To put things in perspective, I've got two different pistons

333
00:19:32,101 --> 00:19:33,540
for you to look at.

334
00:19:33,541 --> 00:19:36,940
This is a piston out of a modern Class 8

335
00:19:36,941 --> 00:19:39,140
over-the-road truck engine, the kind you'd see

336
00:19:39,141 --> 00:19:41,440
racing down the interstate highway.

337
00:19:41,441 --> 00:19:45,560
This piston will produce 112 horsepower in that engine.

338
00:19:45,561 --> 00:19:50,060
By comparison, this is a piston out of the GE AC 6000 engine.

339
00:19:50,061 --> 00:19:54,060
This piston in that engine will produce 375 horsepower.

340
00:19:56,600 --> 00:19:58,560
Although called a diesel locomotive,

341
00:19:58,561 --> 00:20:01,800
the engine is really a combination of power plants.

342
00:20:01,801 --> 00:20:05,140
Most people don't understand that a diesel locomotive is

343
00:20:05,141 --> 00:20:08,440
actually a diesel electric locomotive.

344
00:20:08,441 --> 00:20:11,080
The diesel engine produces horsepower,

345
00:20:11,081 --> 00:20:13,020
and it drives an alternator, which

346
00:20:13,021 --> 00:20:15,120
produces electrical energy.

347
00:20:15,121 --> 00:20:18,360
That electrical energy is then controlled and fed down

348
00:20:18,361 --> 00:20:22,300
to six traction motors, one on each axle,

349
00:20:22,301 --> 00:20:25,660
and that's where the pulling power actually is produced.

350
00:20:25,661 --> 00:20:29,060
The locomotive uses AC, or alternating current, traction

351
00:20:29,061 --> 00:20:32,520
motors, which are very efficient and reduce wheel slippage

352
00:20:32,521 --> 00:20:35,360
significantly.

353
00:20:35,361 --> 00:20:38,500
In the world of railroading, wheel slippage is to be avoided

354
00:20:38,501 --> 00:20:40,260
because it reduces speed and power.

355
00:20:44,800 --> 00:20:48,360
By using separate AC motors on each axle of the locomotive

356
00:20:48,361 --> 00:20:52,160
and monitoring them through a central control computer system,

357
00:20:52,161 --> 00:20:54,600
the designers have ingeniously and significantly

358
00:20:54,601 --> 00:20:58,100
the train's traction, a key ingredient to the rail giant's

359
00:20:58,101 --> 00:21:00,100
success.

360
00:21:00,101 --> 00:21:04,100
So each of the six axles on the AC6000 is controlled

361
00:21:04,101 --> 00:21:07,100
individually from the other five.

362
00:21:07,101 --> 00:21:11,100
In that manner, when one wheel has a tendency to slip, it can

363
00:21:11,101 --> 00:21:14,100
be controlled, and the other five axles can pick up some of the

364
00:21:14,101 --> 00:21:17,600
load temporarily until the locomotive passes across that

365
00:21:17,601 --> 00:21:21,100
slippery section of track.

366
00:21:21,101 --> 00:21:24,100
There are only 106 of these Titans operating around

367
00:21:24,101 --> 00:21:26,300
around the world today.

368
00:21:26,400 --> 00:21:29,400
They are the locomotives that are called in when only the biggest

369
00:21:29,500 --> 00:21:31,700
and the strongest can get the job done.

370
00:21:35,240 --> 00:21:38,140
On the water, the biggest and strongest of ships

371
00:21:38,240 --> 00:21:42,360
are specialty ships, giants that are rewriting the record books

372
00:21:42,460 --> 00:21:43,760
for size and innovation.

373
00:21:48,360 --> 00:21:50,500
Big Boy steam locomotives were generating

374
00:21:50,501 --> 00:21:54,240
in excess of 6,000 horsepower in 1941.

375
00:21:54,340 --> 00:21:58,040
It wasn't until 1969 that a diesel electric locomotive

376
00:21:58,140 --> 00:22:01,740
was built that could produce more than 6,000 horsepower,

377
00:22:01,840 --> 00:22:04,640
the Union Pacific DD-A40X Centennial.

378
00:22:12,360 --> 00:22:14,200
Over 2 thirds of the Earth's surface

379
00:22:14,300 --> 00:22:16,560
is covered with water.

380
00:22:16,660 --> 00:22:18,500
The monarchs of this aquatic domain

381
00:22:18,501 --> 00:22:22,300
ships. They come in all shapes and sizes,

382
00:22:22,301 --> 00:22:28,180
and all have specialized tasks, from military giants to cruise

383
00:22:28,181 --> 00:22:34,740
ships to oil tankers and huge cargo carriers.

384
00:22:34,741 --> 00:22:37,980
The world of big machines on the high seas is one of great

385
00:22:37,981 --> 00:22:42,860
diversity. Some of the largest and most

386
00:22:42,861 --> 00:22:45,900
impressive of these marine engineering wonders are the

387
00:22:45,901 --> 00:22:50,540
heavy lift ships. When it comes to piggybacking

388
00:22:50,640 --> 00:22:54,440
huge cargoes on the world's oceans, shipping companies, oil

389
00:22:54,540 --> 00:22:58,440
drillers, and the United States Navy turn to these giants of the

390
00:22:58,540 --> 00:23:04,480
seas, including Dockwise Shipping's massive Blue

391
00:23:04,580 --> 00:23:08,720
Marlin. The Blue Marlin is indeed

392
00:23:08,820 --> 00:23:12,700
the largest heavy lift ship in the world by a considerable

393
00:23:12,701 --> 00:23:19,500
She's 737 feet long overall and 206 feet wide.

394
00:23:19,501 --> 00:23:22,140
The ingenious process by which heavy objects

395
00:23:22,141 --> 00:23:25,740
are loaded onto the huge ship is one of the marvels of modern

396
00:23:25,741 --> 00:23:26,940
naval engineering.

397
00:23:26,941 --> 00:23:31,660
Here, the Blue Marlin prepares to transport the massive

398
00:23:31,661 --> 00:23:35,980
Thunder Horse oil drilling and production platform from Korea

399
00:23:35,981 --> 00:23:37,660
to the Gulf of Mexico.

400
00:23:37,661 --> 00:23:40,560
Mexico.

401
00:23:40,660 --> 00:23:46,160
The Thunder Horse is definitely the largest oil and gas

402
00:23:46,260 --> 00:23:50,700
floating offshore platform ever built.

403
00:23:50,800 --> 00:23:53,440
It will weigh, at the time we transport it,

404
00:23:53,540 --> 00:23:57,840
some 60,000 metric tons.

405
00:23:57,940 --> 00:24:00,180
Key to the loading process is the ship's ability

406
00:24:00,280 --> 00:24:02,780
to partially self-submerge.

407
00:24:02,880 --> 00:24:07,020
It takes about four hours to fully submerge the deck of the

408
00:24:07,021 --> 00:24:11,520
by adding ballast water to ballast tanks within the ship

409
00:24:11,620 --> 00:24:13,760
to safely submerge it.

410
00:24:13,860 --> 00:24:16,800
And you'll note on the aft end of the vessel

411
00:24:16,900 --> 00:24:20,060
are two large, what we call, buoyancy towers.

412
00:24:20,160 --> 00:24:22,340
These towers also contain ballast tanks,

413
00:24:22,440 --> 00:24:25,180
which we will fill with water, which enable the ship

414
00:24:25,280 --> 00:24:27,380
to submerge on an even keel.

415
00:24:32,120 --> 00:24:34,380
Maneuvering the giant oil platform into place

416
00:24:34,381 --> 00:24:37,880
is a delicate operation, even though it involves thousands

417
00:24:37,881 --> 00:24:42,620
of tons of equipment and thousands of horsepower.

418
00:24:42,621 --> 00:24:45,260
In using tugboats, the floating cargo

419
00:24:45,261 --> 00:24:47,920
is brought over the deck of our ship,

420
00:24:47,921 --> 00:24:50,860
whereupon we attach winch wires to the cargo

421
00:24:50,861 --> 00:24:53,900
from winch towers on the vessel.

422
00:24:53,901 --> 00:24:57,240
We then slowly bring the cargo over the deck of the ship

423
00:24:57,241 --> 00:24:59,240
into precisely the right position.

424
00:25:01,880 --> 00:25:03,580
The final part of the operation

425
00:25:03,581 --> 00:25:07,180
comes when the Blue Marlin is resurfaced to a proper draft.

426
00:25:11,980 --> 00:25:14,620
Perhaps the Blue Marlin's greatest moment of glory

427
00:25:14,720 --> 00:25:17,360
came when she was chosen to ferry the stricken Navy

428
00:25:17,460 --> 00:25:20,960
destroyer USS Cole back to the United States

429
00:25:21,060 --> 00:25:23,360
after it was attacked by terrorists in Yemen

430
00:25:23,460 --> 00:25:27,200
in October of 2000.

431
00:25:27,300 --> 00:25:29,880
The hole in the side of the Cole was very big

432
00:25:29,881 --> 00:25:33,780
and made the vessel unseaworthy.

433
00:25:33,781 --> 00:25:37,220
So basically, the challenge in loading the USS Cole

434
00:25:37,221 --> 00:25:38,880
was not so much the weight of the ship,

435
00:25:38,881 --> 00:25:43,420
because a destroyer like that weighs about 8,500 tons,

436
00:25:43,421 --> 00:25:45,920
which for the Blue Marlin is a relatively

437
00:25:45,921 --> 00:25:47,940
insignificant amount of weight.

438
00:25:47,941 --> 00:25:52,340
The challenge really was more how do you put a long,

439
00:25:52,341 --> 00:25:55,880
narrow body like the Cole onto the top of our ship

440
00:25:55,881 --> 00:25:59,880
and stabilize it so that it doesn't tip over.

441
00:25:59,881 --> 00:26:03,880
The transport of the coal was very smooth, very uneventful

442
00:26:03,881 --> 00:26:07,880
voyage, very successful, I would say, on the part of Dockwise

443
00:26:07,881 --> 00:26:08,880
and the US Navy.

444
00:26:14,880 --> 00:26:19,880
Another class of ocean giant that boggles the mind is that

445
00:26:19,881 --> 00:26:20,880
of oil drill ships.

446
00:26:20,881 --> 00:26:23,880
The biggest of them is Transocean's Discoverer

447
00:26:23,881 --> 00:26:24,880
Enterprise.

448
00:26:24,881 --> 00:26:28,880
The ship's 835 feet long, 125 feet long,

449
00:26:28,881 --> 00:26:34,720
125 feet wide. The Derrick Tower is more than 375 feet over the

450
00:26:34,721 --> 00:26:40,560
surface of the water above the ridge. Drill ships ply the oceans,

451
00:26:40,561 --> 00:26:44,800
exploring their depths for oil and gas fields. As oil reserves dwindle

452
00:26:44,801 --> 00:26:48,800
onshore and along coastlines, oil companies are turning more and more

453
00:26:48,801 --> 00:26:53,440
to deep sea exploration. The need for additional reserves and

454
00:26:53,441 --> 00:26:58,480
oil and gas production has driven the industry into ultra deep water.

455
00:26:58,481 --> 00:27:02,060
In order to satisfy our clients and reach these new frontiers,

456
00:27:02,640 --> 00:27:06,040
we have to continue to develop bigger and better tools.

457
00:27:06,460 --> 00:27:09,920
The Discoverer Enterprise's hull was built at the Estano shipyard

458
00:27:09,921 --> 00:27:11,140
in El Farol, Spain.

459
00:27:11,780 --> 00:27:14,720
She was completed in Pascagoula, Mississippi in 1999.

460
00:27:15,900 --> 00:27:17,040
The ship isn't just big.

461
00:27:17,960 --> 00:27:18,960
She's revolutionary.

462
00:27:19,680 --> 00:27:22,480
We decided to throw out all the conventional thinking

463
00:27:22,481 --> 00:27:24,760
and start all over with a clean sheet of paper.

464
00:27:25,640 --> 00:27:27,740
Part of that process led to the development

465
00:27:27,741 --> 00:27:29,420
of what we call dual activity drilling,

466
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:33,440
where we utilize two complete sets of equipment on one rig

467
00:27:33,441 --> 00:27:36,700
to be able to drill two wells simultaneously.

468
00:27:37,900 --> 00:27:40,700
The ship is so big because it has to accommodate

469
00:27:40,701 --> 00:27:42,420
two complete sets of drilling equipment.

470
00:27:43,100 --> 00:27:44,820
This dual drilling capability

471
00:27:44,821 --> 00:27:46,980
allows the Discoverer Enterprise's clients

472
00:27:46,981 --> 00:27:50,420
to save up to 40% developing oil fields

473
00:27:50,421 --> 00:27:52,620
because it speeds up the whole process.

474
00:27:53,780 --> 00:27:55,900
The ship often operates in such deep water

475
00:27:55,901 --> 00:27:58,600
that anchoring while drilling is impractical.

476
00:27:59,960 --> 00:28:03,360
Instead, an ingenious high-tech stabilizing system

477
00:28:03,361 --> 00:28:04,061
has been developed.

478
00:28:05,620 --> 00:28:07,760
We use what's called dynamic positioning.

479
00:28:08,280 --> 00:28:11,540
Dynamic positioning consists of computer-controlled thrusters

480
00:28:11,541 --> 00:28:14,920
that have inputs from satellites for positioning.

481
00:28:15,580 --> 00:28:19,060
We take inputs from satellites to determine what our position is.

482
00:28:19,061 --> 00:28:22,880
We then use a computer to very tightly control the position of the vessel

483
00:28:22,881 --> 00:28:26,120
using six, 7,000 horsepower thrusters.

484
00:28:26,220 --> 00:28:29,360
The system is so efficient that it can hold the ship steady

485
00:28:29,361 --> 00:28:31,580
in gales up to 70 miles per hour.

486
00:28:32,220 --> 00:28:35,860
In very high winds, in fact, we're able to maintain location

487
00:28:35,861 --> 00:28:40,840
within a few tens of feet in thousands and thousands of feet

488
00:28:40,841 --> 00:28:43,320
of water. All of this combined technology

489
00:28:43,321 --> 00:28:47,560
adds up to a truly amazing ship, one that is capable of digging

490
00:28:47,561 --> 00:28:50,640
to incredible depths. The ship is capable of drilling

491
00:28:50,641 --> 00:28:53,240
in 10,000 feet of water or more.

492
00:28:53,340 --> 00:28:55,740
In addition, even in that water depth,

493
00:28:55,840 --> 00:28:58,040
we can drill wells that are nearly five miles deep

494
00:28:58,140 --> 00:29:00,440
below the seaford.

495
00:29:00,540 --> 00:29:03,080
Oil drill ships may be the champs for digging deep

496
00:29:03,180 --> 00:29:05,280
into the earth beneath the sea.

497
00:29:05,380 --> 00:29:07,400
But when it comes to digging on land,

498
00:29:07,500 --> 00:29:09,700
nothing can top the giant excavators.

499
00:29:14,240 --> 00:29:16,200
The engines aboard the Discoverer Enterprise

500
00:29:16,300 --> 00:29:19,700
can generate enough power to serve approximately 40,000

501
00:29:19,701 --> 00:29:20,401
homes.

502
00:29:27,580 --> 00:29:30,040
When it comes to supersized machines,

503
00:29:30,041 --> 00:29:32,380
giant Earth movers are at the starting point

504
00:29:32,381 --> 00:29:34,020
of our world economy.

505
00:29:34,021 --> 00:29:37,160
Without them, it just would not function.

506
00:29:37,161 --> 00:29:41,860
All raw materials have to be mined to produce the cars we

507
00:29:41,861 --> 00:29:48,100
drive, the streets we drive on, the buildings we work in.

508
00:29:48,101 --> 00:29:50,340
Among the biggest of all Earth movers

509
00:29:50,341 --> 00:29:54,600
are giant bucket wheel excavators,

510
00:29:54,601 --> 00:29:59,380
some reaching over 700 feet in length, 300 feet in height,

511
00:29:59,381 --> 00:30:03,920
and weighing as much as 30 million pounds.

512
00:30:03,921 --> 00:30:06,660
They're the largest machines on land capable of moving

513
00:30:06,661 --> 00:30:07,720
under their own power.

514
00:30:11,100 --> 00:30:13,760
Bucket wheel excavators, or BWEs,

515
00:30:13,761 --> 00:30:16,760
are used primarily to mine lignite or brown coal.

516
00:30:18,100 --> 00:30:22,000
It works best in loose, sandy material.

517
00:30:22,100 --> 00:30:24,840
The digging wheel and its multi buckets

518
00:30:24,940 --> 00:30:27,940
is not designed for digging through solid rock

519
00:30:28,040 --> 00:30:30,020
or glacier-type boulders.

520
00:30:30,120 --> 00:30:34,980
It needs looser, more gravelly-type material

521
00:30:35,080 --> 00:30:38,420
to dig through efficiently.

522
00:30:38,520 --> 00:30:40,320
This giant erector set of a machine

523
00:30:40,420 --> 00:30:42,820
is made up of many components.

524
00:30:42,920 --> 00:30:46,000
You have the large digging wheel at the front,

525
00:30:46,001 --> 00:30:48,700
which is 71 feet in diameter.

526
00:30:48,800 --> 00:30:51,800
It is connected to a large boom, which then

527
00:30:51,900 --> 00:30:54,340
connects to the central tower.

528
00:30:56,780 --> 00:31:01,140
The central tower has a conveyor bridge, which

529
00:31:01,240 --> 00:31:03,060
extends from the back end of that.

530
00:31:03,160 --> 00:31:05,520
And then that is hooked to what is referred

531
00:31:05,620 --> 00:31:07,560
to as a discharge unit.

532
00:31:07,660 --> 00:31:10,760
The discharge unit carries the material

533
00:31:10,860 --> 00:31:15,460
onto large overland conveyors.

534
00:31:15,461 --> 00:31:18,260
Although incredibly efficient as earth movers,

535
00:31:18,360 --> 00:31:23,700
BWE's pose no threat to the world's land speed record.

536
00:31:23,800 --> 00:31:27,580
The speed of these machines are up to perhaps maybe

537
00:31:27,680 --> 00:31:30,540
about 30 feet per minute, which would translate

538
00:31:30,640 --> 00:31:35,840
into about 1 third of a mile per hour.

539
00:31:35,940 --> 00:31:37,820
However, the fact that they can move at all

540
00:31:37,920 --> 00:31:40,880
is a technological miracle brought about through the use

541
00:31:40,980 --> 00:31:43,780
of giant crawlers.

542
00:31:43,781 --> 00:31:47,480
The main tower assembly is supported by an undercarriage

543
00:31:47,580 --> 00:31:53,820
with 12 massive crawlers, each 49 feet in length.

544
00:31:53,920 --> 00:31:57,160
The reason that these bucket wheel excavators, like 293,

545
00:31:57,260 --> 00:32:02,660
require so many crawlers is for flotation.

546
00:32:02,760 --> 00:32:06,040
Because of the underfooting that the machine works on,

547
00:32:06,140 --> 00:32:11,840
it has to be extremely level, extremely stable.

548
00:32:11,841 --> 00:32:15,280
computers allow the operators to monitor all aspects of the digging

549
00:32:15,380 --> 00:32:16,380
operation.

550
00:32:16,480 --> 00:32:19,040
But even with this technology, several crew members

551
00:32:19,140 --> 00:32:21,980
are needed to operate the machine.

552
00:32:22,080 --> 00:32:25,980
These machines typically run by about four or five people.

553
00:32:26,080 --> 00:32:29,200
There is one operator that is located

554
00:32:29,300 --> 00:32:31,600
in the main operator's cab.

555
00:32:31,700 --> 00:32:34,260
This operator would have an overall control

556
00:32:34,360 --> 00:32:37,600
of the whole machine.

557
00:32:37,700 --> 00:32:39,340
The price tag for one of these brutes

558
00:32:39,341 --> 00:32:42,680
is as big as the machine itself.

559
00:32:42,780 --> 00:32:44,640
The bucket wheel excavator of this size

560
00:32:44,740 --> 00:32:47,580
costs over $100 million.

561
00:32:47,680 --> 00:32:49,180
The weight of the machine, obviously,

562
00:32:49,280 --> 00:32:52,720
a major factor in determining the cost of the machine

563
00:32:52,820 --> 00:32:57,960
and the sophistication of the equipment.

564
00:32:58,060 --> 00:33:02,160
Yet if you want to move an astonishing 314,000 cubic yards

565
00:33:02,260 --> 00:33:06,040
of material in one day, a giant bucket wheel excavator

566
00:33:06,140 --> 00:33:09,100
is the only way to go.

567
00:33:09,101 --> 00:33:11,840
Another goliath amongst earth-moving machines

568
00:33:11,940 --> 00:33:15,940
is the Tarex ONK RH-400 hydraulic front shovel

569
00:33:16,040 --> 00:33:17,840
excavator.

570
00:33:17,940 --> 00:33:23,440
The RH-400 is the world's largest front shovel.

571
00:33:23,540 --> 00:33:29,460
57 cubic yard bucket, 4,400 horsepower, 1,100 tons

572
00:33:29,560 --> 00:33:30,460
operating.

573
00:33:30,560 --> 00:33:33,660
It is simply huge.

574
00:33:33,760 --> 00:33:37,660
There has never been another hydraulic excavator

575
00:33:37,661 --> 00:33:41,940
excavator like the RH400.

576
00:33:41,941 --> 00:33:44,740
Everything kind of looks like it's out of a science fiction

577
00:33:44,741 --> 00:33:47,800
novel or something.

578
00:33:47,801 --> 00:33:50,480
With its gigantic bucket, it's able to do the work

579
00:33:50,481 --> 00:33:53,300
of many smaller excavators in less time

580
00:33:53,301 --> 00:33:55,840
and with more efficiency.

581
00:33:55,841 --> 00:34:00,020
The RH400's large bucket is capable of loading

582
00:34:00,021 --> 00:34:05,060
a 240-ton capacity truck, three quick passes,

583
00:34:05,061 --> 00:34:07,460
averaging less than 29 seconds.

584
00:34:11,460 --> 00:34:14,400
One of the main advantages of a hydraulic diesel shovel

585
00:34:14,500 --> 00:34:16,500
is its mobility.

586
00:34:16,600 --> 00:34:19,100
Unlike some electric shovels tethered to power lines,

587
00:34:19,200 --> 00:34:21,640
it can move freely.

588
00:34:21,740 --> 00:34:24,540
Once it's finished working at a location,

589
00:34:24,640 --> 00:34:28,820
it can relatively quickly move to another working location

590
00:34:28,920 --> 00:34:30,020
and go right to work.

591
00:34:32,560 --> 00:34:34,520
Mining companies thinking of buying the job

592
00:34:34,521 --> 00:34:37,720
can also expect to shovel out a lot of cash.

593
00:34:37,820 --> 00:34:41,500
They have to be ready to write a big check for, oh,

594
00:34:41,600 --> 00:34:44,300
in the neighborhood of $10 to $11 million.

595
00:34:47,140 --> 00:34:52,180
But because of their size and their massive capacities,

596
00:34:52,280 --> 00:34:55,980
their productivity should outweigh the cost of the machine

597
00:34:56,080 --> 00:34:57,580
in the long run.

598
00:34:57,680 --> 00:35:01,020
And speaking of long runs, in the world of publishing,

599
00:35:01,120 --> 00:35:02,940
huge new printing presses are bringing

600
00:35:02,941 --> 00:35:05,700
printing presses are bringing a whole new meaning to the words

601
00:35:05,701 --> 00:35:09,480
power of the press.

602
00:35:09,481 --> 00:35:12,580
Except for maintenance days, bucket wheel excavators work

603
00:35:12,581 --> 00:35:17,380
seven days a week, 24 hours a day, 365 days a year.

604
00:35:22,300 --> 00:35:24,600
Possibly more than any other invention,

605
00:35:24,601 --> 00:35:27,200
the printing press changed the world.

606
00:35:27,201 --> 00:35:31,740
And now they're among the world's biggest machines.

607
00:35:31,741 --> 00:35:34,640
of the press, written information was available only

608
00:35:34,641 --> 00:35:38,640
to an elite few who read books that had been written by hand.

609
00:35:41,140 --> 00:35:44,140
Johannes Gutenberg developed the first press with movable type

610
00:35:44,141 --> 00:35:49,640
in Germany around 1440. After its invention, knowledge

611
00:35:49,641 --> 00:35:52,640
about science, literature, technology, and journalism

612
00:35:52,641 --> 00:35:56,340
became available to everyone and spread across the globe.

613
00:35:56,341 --> 00:36:02,340
Today's presses are giant machines, often housed in

614
00:36:02,440 --> 00:36:05,920
buildings acres in size, and capable of printing billions

615
00:36:06,020 --> 00:36:07,320
of pages per week.

616
00:36:11,720 --> 00:36:15,600
The new $72 million press system for the Indianapolis Star

617
00:36:15,700 --> 00:36:19,500
newspaper is one of the most advanced in the country.

618
00:36:19,600 --> 00:36:23,400
This is one of the largest press systems in the world.

619
00:36:23,401 --> 00:36:28,240
It stretches almost 400 feet in length and is 65 feet high.

620
00:36:28,340 --> 00:36:32,840
It's made up of 24 towers and four locations with folders.

621
00:36:32,940 --> 00:36:35,540
There's actually, it can actually be run as four

622
00:36:35,640 --> 00:36:39,780
individual presses, or it can be run as a series of presses

623
00:36:39,880 --> 00:36:42,620
connected, and that's the reason it's all in one long line.

624
00:36:47,520 --> 00:36:49,500
The first step in putting news to print

625
00:36:49,600 --> 00:36:51,700
comes when stories are electronically transmitted

626
00:36:51,701 --> 00:36:54,540
transmitted to the press site from the Star's downtown

627
00:36:54,640 --> 00:36:57,640
Indianapolis newsroom.

628
00:36:57,740 --> 00:37:00,200
Once that page is transmitted out here,

629
00:37:00,300 --> 00:37:04,140
we output the page through a film processor that provides us

630
00:37:04,240 --> 00:37:08,320
with a large, full-size page negative that we then take and

631
00:37:08,420 --> 00:37:10,980
move over to our plate-making machines.

632
00:37:11,080 --> 00:37:14,360
The plate-making machines then have light that exposes the

633
00:37:14,460 --> 00:37:17,660
negative onto the plate, and the plate then has the finished

634
00:37:17,760 --> 00:37:19,700
image that we want to reproduce.

635
00:37:19,701 --> 00:37:22,600
The plate is then moved out into the press room

636
00:37:22,700 --> 00:37:25,640
and put on the cylinders of the press in preparation

637
00:37:25,740 --> 00:37:27,240
for printing the product.

638
00:37:30,040 --> 00:37:33,040
Next, a web of newsprint is threaded through the press

639
00:37:33,140 --> 00:37:35,140
in preparation for the printing process.

640
00:37:39,560 --> 00:37:41,760
Then the giant machine is brought to life

641
00:37:41,860 --> 00:37:42,760
with a push of a button.

642
00:37:46,160 --> 00:37:48,960
The entire system is completely computerized.

643
00:37:48,961 --> 00:37:52,260
Everything on the press can be controlled from this location.

644
00:37:52,360 --> 00:37:54,260
As the web passes through the press,

645
00:37:54,360 --> 00:37:56,260
there are sensors that tell us what

646
00:37:56,360 --> 00:37:57,960
the tension is of the paper.

647
00:37:58,060 --> 00:38:01,300
The better the tension and the more accurate we run the tension,

648
00:38:01,400 --> 00:38:04,900
the better the reproduction as the paper goes through the press.

649
00:38:05,000 --> 00:38:07,020
Feeding this giant beast with newsprint

650
00:38:07,120 --> 00:38:09,520
is a never-ending task.

651
00:38:09,620 --> 00:38:11,820
This is our newsprint supply warehouse.

652
00:38:11,920 --> 00:38:15,960
We have a 25-day supply of newspaper in this warehouse.

653
00:38:16,060 --> 00:38:18,920
Each one of the rolls will unwind to about a little

654
00:38:18,960 --> 00:38:20,620
miles worth of newsprint.

655
00:38:24,000 --> 00:38:25,960
Deep in the bowels of the press building,

656
00:38:26,060 --> 00:38:28,300
a complex automated system has been built

657
00:38:28,400 --> 00:38:31,200
to get the giant newsprint rolls to the press as quickly

658
00:38:31,300 --> 00:38:32,600
and safely as possible.

659
00:38:37,420 --> 00:38:41,920
The awesome machine's demand for ink is also insatiable.

660
00:38:42,020 --> 00:38:43,920
This is our ink tank farm.

661
00:38:44,020 --> 00:38:48,960
We've got six large tanks here, two 7,500-gallon black

662
00:38:48,961 --> 00:38:53,460
tanks and four 2,500-gallon tanks that we use for our blue,

663
00:38:53,461 --> 00:38:56,000
red, yellow, and spot red colors.

664
00:38:56,001 --> 00:38:58,960
During the course of the year, we'll use over 200,000 gallons

665
00:38:58,961 --> 00:39:01,300
of ink.

666
00:39:01,301 --> 00:39:03,600
One unexpected byproduct that resulted

667
00:39:03,601 --> 00:39:06,020
from the installation of the new presses

668
00:39:06,021 --> 00:39:09,520
was the amount of climbing workers had to do to maintain them.

669
00:39:09,521 --> 00:39:14,060
I would say that overall, our operators consistently

670
00:39:14,061 --> 00:39:17,820
lost a few pounds each moving to working on these presses

671
00:39:17,821 --> 00:39:21,720
is because of the size of them.

672
00:39:21,820 --> 00:39:26,860
Papers rumble through the press at up to 25 miles per hour.

673
00:39:26,960 --> 00:39:31,660
Yet it's so well engineered, the vibration is minimal.

674
00:39:31,760 --> 00:39:33,800
We can take a penny and balance a penny on edge

675
00:39:33,900 --> 00:39:37,300
on the side frame of the press when it's running 75,000 an hour,

676
00:39:37,400 --> 00:39:39,880
and it will stay standing up for as long as you leave it there.

677
00:39:43,840 --> 00:39:45,340
After the papers are printed,

678
00:39:45,341 --> 00:39:48,680
they're automatically sent to the packaging department.

679
00:39:48,681 --> 00:39:51,840
Now the gripper conveyor is like a series of hands

680
00:39:51,841 --> 00:39:55,080
that have fingers on it that pick up each paper individually

681
00:39:55,081 --> 00:39:57,780
and then take those papers to our mailroom department

682
00:39:57,781 --> 00:39:59,080
or our packaging department.

683
00:39:59,081 --> 00:40:01,580
In our packaging department, we would take those papers,

684
00:40:01,581 --> 00:40:04,700
we'd drop them off and put them into another

685
00:40:04,701 --> 00:40:06,800
in-feed conveyor where they go into a stacker

686
00:40:06,801 --> 00:40:08,900
where stacks of newspapers are created.

687
00:40:08,901 --> 00:40:11,140
We put a tie around those papers,

688
00:40:11,141 --> 00:40:15,340
then they get sent out to our distribution centers.

689
00:40:15,341 --> 00:40:19,680
With a possible combined output of 300,000 copies per hour,

690
00:40:19,780 --> 00:40:23,340
this giant printing press system adds some real mass

691
00:40:23,440 --> 00:40:24,980
to the notion of communication.

692
00:40:28,980 --> 00:40:33,700
Of course, another media titan is the television.

693
00:40:33,800 --> 00:40:36,420
From the days when screens were only inches wide

694
00:40:36,520 --> 00:40:41,500
to today's mammoth screens, it's a technological wonder.

695
00:40:41,600 --> 00:40:44,800
Giant LED, or light-emitting diode screens,

696
00:40:44,801 --> 00:40:49,600
have become fixtures throughout the world.

697
00:40:49,700 --> 00:40:52,400
The largest continuous LED screen in the world

698
00:40:52,500 --> 00:40:56,680
is located at the capital of all things extreme and extravagant,

699
00:40:56,780 --> 00:40:59,940
Las Vegas, Nevada.

700
00:41:00,040 --> 00:41:04,660
By day, thousands of tourists pass right under it,

701
00:41:04,760 --> 00:41:07,660
many of them unaware that this giant canopy covering the city's

702
00:41:07,760 --> 00:41:11,460
Fremont Street transforms into one of the wonders of the

703
00:41:11,560 --> 00:41:14,800
electronic age by night.

704
00:41:14,801 --> 00:41:21,800
It's called VivaVision, and it's the star attraction

705
00:41:21,801 --> 00:41:25,800
of the Fremont Street Experience in downtown Las Vegas.

706
00:41:25,801 --> 00:41:28,800
It's close to five football fields long.

707
00:41:28,801 --> 00:41:30,800
It's 1,500 feet in length.

708
00:41:30,801 --> 00:41:32,800
It's 90 feet up in the sky.

709
00:41:32,801 --> 00:41:35,800
There's over 12 million lights on the canopy.

710
00:41:35,801 --> 00:41:39,800
It's supported by 550,000 watts of sound.

711
00:41:39,801 --> 00:41:42,800
So when we say it's the biggest, biggest, biggest,

712
00:41:42,801 --> 00:41:45,440
So when we say it's the biggest big screen on the planet,

713
00:41:45,540 --> 00:41:49,440
there's just nothing like it anywhere in the world.

714
00:41:49,540 --> 00:41:51,240
At the heart of the gigantic display

715
00:41:51,340 --> 00:41:53,940
are the tiny LED lamps.

716
00:41:54,040 --> 00:41:56,640
LED is an acronym for light-emitting diode.

717
00:41:56,740 --> 00:42:00,160
A light-emitting diode more or less houses a microchip,

718
00:42:00,260 --> 00:42:03,160
in a sense, inside a lens.

719
00:42:06,060 --> 00:42:08,860
Through the wizardry of VivaVision,

720
00:42:08,960 --> 00:42:11,460
millions of the LEDs are orchestrated

721
00:42:11,461 --> 00:42:14,300
using the mechanics of television

722
00:42:14,400 --> 00:42:19,360
to create the giant screen and its colorful display.

723
00:42:19,460 --> 00:42:22,400
This is our red LED.

724
00:42:22,500 --> 00:42:25,100
And this is our green.

725
00:42:25,200 --> 00:42:27,000
And this is our blue.

726
00:42:27,100 --> 00:42:29,620
When we bring all three colors to full,

727
00:42:29,720 --> 00:42:32,020
that gives us a white pixel.

728
00:42:32,120 --> 00:42:35,520
Now here, we have the LEDs on at full power.

729
00:42:35,620 --> 00:42:38,020
When we're using them in functional display with Viva

730
00:42:38,021 --> 00:42:42,520
VivaVision, we mix and match voltages.

731
00:42:42,521 --> 00:42:46,660
Each one of these LEDs has 256 levels of dimming capability,

732
00:42:46,661 --> 00:42:49,720
thus giving us our 16.7 million color combinations.

733
00:42:53,680 --> 00:42:56,320
The whole project emanates from a master control room

734
00:42:56,321 --> 00:43:00,480
located high above Fremont Street.

735
00:43:00,481 --> 00:43:02,420
All aspects of VivaVision are controlled

736
00:43:02,421 --> 00:43:05,660
by a massive bank of computers.

737
00:43:05,661 --> 00:43:11,200
VivaVision works with an array of eight computers that power

738
00:43:11,201 --> 00:43:14,060
each section of the canopy itself.

739
00:43:14,061 --> 00:43:17,900
So within those eight computers are a number of hard drives

740
00:43:17,901 --> 00:43:22,340
and lots and lots of storage to accommodate these large show

741
00:43:22,341 --> 00:43:25,520
files.

742
00:43:25,521 --> 00:43:29,180
The $17 million VivaVision screen

743
00:43:29,181 --> 00:43:32,180
programs everything from animation to live action

744
00:43:32,181 --> 00:43:33,880
videos and special events.

745
00:43:37,220 --> 00:43:39,480
What was conceived as a dream screen

746
00:43:39,580 --> 00:43:43,420
has become a reality and a resounding hit.

747
00:43:43,520 --> 00:43:46,120
In this town of instant winners and losers,

748
00:43:46,220 --> 00:43:48,940
the Fremont Street Experience Viva Vision screen

749
00:43:49,040 --> 00:43:50,340
is clearly a winner.

750
00:43:53,740 --> 00:43:57,840
And it takes its special place amongst the giants we call

751
00:43:57,940 --> 00:43:59,540
the world's biggest machines.

